// THE BLOCKING LAYER: wait_completion() and the three end-to-end operations
// built on it. Compiled only with wb_dma_cfg_pkg::HAS_BLOCKING.
//
// ============================================================================
// WHY FOUR OPERATIONS SHARE ONE FILE
// ============================================================================
//
// Rule 1 of the coding guidelines gives a function its own file, and these are
// the only functions in this tree that do not get one. Two reasons, and the
// weak one is listed second on purpose:
//
//   * THEY ARE ONE INCLUDABLE UNIT. Rule 1's own test is "would a build ever
//     want this element without its neighbours?" For these four the answer is
//     no, by construction: each of the three operations is a *_start() plus
//     wait_completion(), all four appear and disappear together with one flag,
//     and there is no configuration of this model that has some of them. That
//     is the same argument rule 5.2 makes for a register bank.
//
//   * pssparser cannot spell it the other way. A declaration inside a
//     `compile if` is invisible to every LATER source file -- silently, with
//     `0 errors` and exit 0 (defect 4, docs/pssparser-defects-2026-08-02.md).
//     transfer_single() calls wait_completion(), so one file per function
//     produces a model with the blocking layer quietly missing from the
//     generated SV.
//
// The cost is real and worth stating: you can no longer find transfer_single()
// by looking for transfer_single.pss. src/pss/README.md's operation table is
// how you find it instead. If the defect is fixed and the first reason ever
// stops holding -- an operation that is blocking on some profiles and not
// others -- split this file then, not before.
//
// The NON-BLOCKING halves of all three operations keep their own files:
// transfer_single_start.pss, transfer_list_start.pss, stop_channel_start.pss,
// plus probe_status.pss and check_completion.pss. Those are the firmware API,
// they are separately includable, and they are where the device knowledge is.
// What follows adds only the loop.
import addr_reg_pkg::*;
import wb_dma_cfg_pkg::*;
import wb_dma_regs_pkg::*;
import wb_dma_types_pkg::*;

extend component wb_dma_ch_c {

  compile if (wb_dma_cfg_pkg::HAS_BLOCKING) {

    // ========================================================================
    // wait_completion -- the shared wait point (operation model §3.1)
    // ========================================================================
    //
    // Not an operation in its own right. It is the ONE place in this tree that
    // suspends the caller, and therefore the whole of what the blocking layer
    // adds.
    //
    // Blocking is realised as
    //
    //     repeat { probe_status; block_until_woken }
    //
    // where probe_status() is the same single CSR read check_completion() uses
    // and the wake comes from this channel's `wake` channel, posted by
    // wb_dma_c::notify_irq(). Nothing is edge-triggered: the condition is
    // re-read from the device on every wake, waking threads test their own
    // conditions, and a spurious wake is harmless.
    //
    // THAT SHARING IS THE ARGUMENT FOR THE WHOLE SPLIT. The non-blocking core
    // is not a second implementation to be kept in step with this one -- it is
    // the body of this one, hoisted. A SystemVerilog regression that only ever
    // calls transfer_single() still executes every register access a firmware
    // caller will make, in the same order, so the two paths cannot drift. That
    // is why there is no second regression for the firmware profile.
    //
    // THE WAKE IS A HINT; CHn_CSR IS THE EVENT. The loop is correct for any
    // wake source and any amount of spurious waking. What it depends on is
    // being woken AT ALL -- see the routing note below.
    //
    // THE SIDE-EFFECT PROBLEM (§3.1.1). probe_status() runs an unbounded number
    // of times and it consumes state: reading CHn_CSR clears ERR and the three
    // interrupt-source bits. Why that is safe is recorded on probe_status() and
    // on `inflight` in wb_dma_ch_c.pss. In short: the value is captured by the
    // same read that observes it, the loop exits holding it, and AT MOST ONE
    // OPERATION PER CHANNEL AT A TIME means the polling thread is the sole
    // consumer of those bits. stop_channel() is the one deliberate exception.
    //
    // WHY THE GUARD IS RELEASED HERE. `inflight` was claimed by the *_start()
    // this wait follows, and an operation is over when its completion has been
    // reported -- which is this return. Releasing here rather than in each
    // caller keeps the claim and the release one fact in two places instead of
    // one in three. stop_channel() does not call this function precisely
    // because it never claimed.
    //
    // COST OF A STALE TOKEN, EXACTLY. The loop probes BEFORE it ever blocks, so
    // a `wake` token left over from a previous operation only makes the first
    // get() return immediately: probe, get-immediate, probe, get-blocks. wake's
    // DEPTH of 1 caps the leftover at one, so it cannot accumulate across
    // operations. One extra register read per operation, worst case. There is
    // deliberately no drain step at entry -- it would cost the same read it
    // saves.
    //
    // THE ROUTING PREREQUISITE IS LOAD-BEARING. A test that never called
    // configure_interrupt_routing() raises no interrupt, so nothing calls
    // notify_irq(), so this blocks forever. notify_irq() posting to every
    // channel blind softens it -- any ONE routed channel's interrupt wakes
    // every waiter -- so a partially routed test still makes progress; a test
    // that routes nothing deadlocks. That is intended. A hang is a more honest
    // result than a green test whose completion path was never real. If you are
    // debugging a hang here, check INT_MSK_A/B before you look at the DMA.
    //
    // The non-blocking profile has no equivalent exposure: it never waits on an
    // interrupt, so a firmware driver that routes nothing still makes progress.
    // The routing requirement belongs to this layer alone.
    target function wb_dma_status_e wait_completion() {
        wb_dma_status_e status;
        bit             tok;
        bool            ok;

        while (true) {
            status = probe_status();
            if (status != WB_DMA_PENDING) {
                break;
            }
            // Not ours yet. get() blocks, and a target exec blocked on a
            // channel operation does not stall other execs on the same
            // executor (§20.8).
            //
            // The token carries no information and is discarded. With
            // CHUNK_DONE enabled the interrupt also fires on events that do NOT
            // complete the operation, and notify_irq() posts to every channel
            // regardless of which one raised it, so most wakes are for someone
            // else. That is exactly why this is a wake-and-recheck loop rather
            // than a single suspend.
            tok = wake.get();
        }

        // The operation is over: drop the claim its *_start() took. The result
        // is discarded because there is one case where the token is
        // legitimately absent -- a *_start() that reported a double-start and
        // returned without arming, whose caller is already in error.
        ok = inflight.try_get(tok);

        return status;
    }

    // ========================================================================
    // transfer_single -- §3.2, group 1, level: end-to-end
    // ========================================================================
    // (`run_transfer` in the first pass; renamed for symmetry with
    // transfer_list.)
    //
    // completes_when: the channel's DONE (or ERR) interrupt has been observed
    //                 and the CHn_CSR read has returned the status and cleared
    //                 the source.
    // waits for:      channel DONE or ERR, on the per-channel interrupt. This
    //                 is the core driver FSM: submit -> RUNNING -> completion.
    //
    // The body is now exactly what the operation always was and nothing more:
    // the two halves, named. All of the device knowledge -- what to program, in
    // what order, which write arms the channel -- is in
    // transfer_single_start(); all of the waiting is in wait_completion(). This
    // function contributes the composition and the return type.
    //
    // TWO CALLERS' OBLIGATIONS THIS SIGNATURE DOES NOT ENCODE, both stated in
    // full on transfer_single_start() and both unchanged by the split:
    //
    //   mode == HW_HANDSHAKE: this call cannot complete on its own. Something
    //   must be calling request_chunk() on the matching wb_dma_hs_c
    //   concurrently (§2.2).
    //
    //   cfg.auto_restart: this call is DELIBERATELY NON-TERMINATING. Another
    //   thread must clear ARS (set_auto_restart(0)) or abort the channel
    //   (stop_channel()). It is a blocking read on a stream that ends when
    //   someone closes it -- so an ARS transfer must never be the only
    //   operation a scenario waits on.
    target function wb_dma_status_e transfer_single(wb_dma_ch_cfg_s cfg) {
        transfer_single_start(cfg);
        return wait_completion();
    }

    // ========================================================================
    // transfer_list -- §3.2, group 3, level: end-to-end
    // ========================================================================
    // (`run_descriptor_chain` in the first pass.)
    //
    // completes_when: a descriptor with EOL set has been processed -- the
    //                 engine stops, sets DONE in CHn_CSR, and raises the
    //                 channel interrupt.
    // waits for:      EOL DONE, plus optional intermediate wakes, on the
    //                 per-channel interrupt. RUNNING has internal structure
    //                 here: per-descriptor progress inside one outstanding
    //                 request.
    //
    // The four-step arming procedure of §3.3 lives in transfer_list_start(),
    // along with the note about which configuration a descriptor does NOT carry
    // and must therefore come from a prior configure_channel().
    //
    // Unlike transfer_single, this completion is unconditional: ARS is
    // documented as meaningless and ignored with external descriptors (§3.3).
    // Two end-to-end operations differing only in completion contract is why
    // neither subsumes the other -- and note that the difference is entirely in
    // the arming half, not in the wait, which is why both share one
    // wait_completion().
    target function wb_dma_status_e transfer_list(addr_handle_t head) {
        transfer_list_start(head);
        return wait_completion();
    }

    // ========================================================================
    // stop_channel -- §3.2, group 1, level: end-to-end
    // ========================================================================
    //
    // completes_when: STOP written; the channel has aborted, ERR is set, and
    //                 the error interrupt (if enabled) has been raised.
    // waits for:      channel ERR, on the per-channel interrupt. An abort path
    //                 that completes the SAME outstanding request from a second
    //                 entry point.
    //
    // An abort is reported as an ERROR, not as a clean stop -- carried into the
    // contract verbatim. Note the consequence for the scenario layer: the
    // aborted transfer_single() also returns, with status WB_DMA_ERROR. Two
    // blocking operations complete off one event.
    //
    // WHY THIS DOES NOT CALL wait_completion(), AND HAS ITS OWN LOOP
    //
    // By definition it aborts a transfer that is still running, so it violates
    // the one-operation-per-channel rule that makes the polling read safe. That
    // exemption used to be a comment; with the `inflight` guard it has to be
    // expressed. stop_channel_start() claims no token, so this function holds
    // none -- and wait_completion() ends by RELEASING one, which would take the
    // aborted transfer's. Hence the loop below: identical to
    // wait_completion()'s except that it does not touch the guard.
    //
    // The duplication is five lines and it is the honest shape. The alternative
    // -- a flag on wait_completion() saying "do not release" -- would hide the
    // one place in this model where two operations legitimately share a
    // channel.
    //
    // The consequences of that sharing are unchanged:
    //
    //   * The read-modify-write in stop_channel_start() reads CHn_CSR, which
    //     clears ERR and the interrupt sources. That read happens BEFORE STOP
    //     is written, so it can only consume status that was already there --
    //     the abort's own ERR is set afterwards and is still seen by the
    //     waiting transfer. What it can lose is a completion that landed in the
    //     instant before the abort.
    //   * Both this call and the aborted transfer_single() then poll the same
    //     CSR, and whichever reads first consumes the ERR. Which of the two
    //     returns the status and which spins is not determined by this model.
    //
    // open (§6.5): is write-1-to-STOP acknowledged before or after the current
    //              WISHBONE cycle retires? That decides whether this operation
    //              can return before the bus is quiet, which matters
    //              post-silicon.
    target function wb_dma_status_e stop_channel() {
        wb_dma_status_e status;
        bit             tok;

        stop_channel_start();

        // wait_completion()'s loop, minus the guard release. See above.
        while (true) {
            status = probe_status();
            if (status != WB_DMA_PENDING) {
                break;
            }
            tok = wake.get();
        }

        return status;
    }

  }
}
